WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.189 --> 00:00:05.860
SpaceX is getting closer to going to Mars
as it tests out prototypes of its gigantic

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.000
Starship nearly every month.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.669
The aim is to fly an uncrewed mission to the
red planet as early as 2024.

00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:17.360
CEO Elon Musk has said he’s fairly confident
people will land on Mars in 2026.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.060
That’s just two years after NASA’s goal
of sending astronauts back to the moon which

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.630
is itself a very ambitious timeline.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:29.410
If and when Starship takes off with travelers
aboard, it won’t just be a trip for those

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.189
with money but those with guts.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.082
It's dangerous, it's uncomfortable. 

00:00:34.082 --> 00:00:38.239
Going to Mars reads like that ad for Shackleton going to the Antarctic.

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:42.390
That’s a reference to the famed Anglo-Irish
explorer Ernest Shackleton, who is said to

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.940
have put out this ad in a newspaper to recruit
brave souls to brave the bitter Antarctic

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:47.940
cold.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.559
Just as the conditions of Antarctica are difficult
and dangerous, the Martian environment won’t

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:53.559
exactly be hospitable.

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:56.739
Honestly, a bunch of people probably will
die in the beginning.

00:00:56.739 --> 00:00:57.739
It’s tough sledding over there.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:02.249
He believes the tough sledding is worth
trying to colonize Mars in case he says anything

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:03.800
were to happen to planet Earth.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.437
Before we talk about the challenges of living
on Mars, it will be tough enough just to get there.

00:01:08.437 --> 00:01:13.050
25 out of the 49 robotic missions to Mars
have failed to get to the fourth planet from

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.050
the Sun.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.410
The journey will take six months or longer.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:20.330
The optimal time to go is when the distance
between the Earth and Mars is shortest.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.870
That’s once every two years or so.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:27.600
Even then, it’s still going to be a 34-mile
or 55 million km trek.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.139
The traveling distance will actually be even
greater because it can’t be done in a straight line.

00:01:32.139 --> 00:01:33.880
So the trajectory will be similar

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.330
to a quarterback throwing a football.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:39.390
They don’t throw the ball to where the receiver
is at any given moment  but where the teammate

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.860
will be in the future as they continue to
run down the field.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.250
Course corrections will be necessary.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.320
If it isn’t done with precision, the spacecraft
could get lost in space or crash when it reaches

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.320
its destination.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.830
It has happened already.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:57.260
In 1998, a software issue sent NASA’s Mars
Climate Orbiter too close to the red planet

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.330
and it was either destroyed in the atmosphere
or got lost orbiting the sun.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:05.520
Live communication won’t be possible either
because it takes as long as 24 minutes for

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.600
radio signals to travel back to Earth.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.590
So the spacecraft is on its own, dependent
on an onboard computer to automate the entire

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:13.690
procedure.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.680
Those minutes will feel like hours.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.080
And once it prepares to land, the vehicle
- this one simulating Starship - will perform

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.200
a complex landing maneuver.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.720
If getting safely to Mars is going to be difficult,
living there will be an even greater challenge.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.930
NASA’s Perseverance rover that touched down
in February has been studying the habitability

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.630
of Mars and looking for signs of past microbial
life.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:42.050
Its cameras captured NASA’s Ingenuity helicopter
flying in the dusty, desert world.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.600
Gigantic dust storms, resembling tornadoes,
can cover the entire planet and the dust is

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.600
toxic.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.280
Many people often assume Mars is hot but because
it’s farther from the sun and has an atmosphere

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.510
100 times thinner than Earth’s, there are
huge temperature swings.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:03.800
A daytime high of 22° Celsius or 71° Fahrenheit
on the surface could drop to -99° Celsius

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.310
or minus 146° Fahrenheit at night.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:11.209
That thin atmosphere would also expose aspiring
Martians to radiation.

00:03:11.209 --> 00:03:16.260
And Mars has no magnetic field to deflect
these deadly rays and particles unlike the

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.709
magnetic bubble that protects Earth.

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:22.810
Building homes underground would offer protection
- or possibly building them above ground with

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.540
a protective layer of regolith - loose deposits
of dust and broken rocks.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.680
There’s no shortage of that on the red planet.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.120
And if you step outside without a spacesuit,
the low atmospheric pressure would cause

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.440
your blood to boil.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.680
A NASA scientist has compared it to popping
a can of soda.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.160
You’d fizz to death.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:43.959
The atmosphere is primarily carbon dioxide
so the failure of a spacesuit is a potential

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:45.180
death sentence.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.250
To get around this problem, NASA is experimenting
with trying to convert CO2 into oxygen with

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.020
a toaster-sized instrument aboard the Perseverance
rover called MOXIE.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:58.640
That could one day provide breathable air
and could also help lift rockets off the planet’s

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.690
surface since propellant depends on oxygen.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.910
And of course, the big challenge of living
on Mars is figuring out how to get safe drinking

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:06.910
water.

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:10.530
There are small quantities of water in the
soil and as vapor in the atmosphere, but most

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.480
of it exists as ice.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.780
Perhaps the most viable option for the first
humans on Mars is to live on their spaceship

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.790
at first, using it as a habitable base for
the building of a city.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:24.240
There’s been talk of terraforming
Mars - engineering it to make it more Earth-like

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.000
by creating enough oxygen so that humans can
breathe.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.310
However, that’s a long way off from the
near-term goal of first-time travelers which

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.371
is simply to survive the night.

00:04:33.371 --> 00:04:34.371
It's dangerous, it's uncomfortable, it's a
long journey.

00:04:34.371 --> 00:04:35.371
You might not come back alive.

00:04:35.371 --> 00:04:38.921
But it's a glorious adventure, and it'll be
an amazing experience. And your name will go in history.

00:04:38.921 --> 00:04:40.111
Yes. You might die.

00:04:40.111 --> 00:04:43.650
If you’re considering whether to book that
ticket to Mars and can’t decide, you can

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.970
learn more about the red planet by watching
space documentaries.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:51.520
But for explorers and dreamers, the red planet
is warm and inviting.

